title "Rehearsal letters inside containers"
key c major

part melody {
  clef bass
  section A {
    c4@mark("A") d e f | tuplet 3/2 { g4@mark("B") a b } c4 d
  }
  // ⚠️ The marks: each of these three used to continue the bar above in ONE music stream,
  // so its octave came from the note before it. A section starts a fresh frame at the
  // part's anchor, so the three notes carry the ' that the stream used to give them - the
  // pitches are D4 / E4 / F4 as before, checked with `lysc check --pitches`.
  section Body { d'1 | }
  section End1 { e'1@mark("C") }
  section End2 { f'1@mark("D") }
}

form main { ~A |: ~Body [1. ~End1] :| [2. ~End2] }

score main "rehearsal-marks-inside-containers" {
  staff melody
  tab melody
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ⚠️ THIS NOTE IS AT THE FILE END ON PURPOSE. A comment ABOVE the music shifts every
// source offset after it, so the whole book's data-pos moves and its snapshot has to be
// re-based -- a diff that proves nothing and hides the next real one.
//
// Four letters, and only ONE of them (A) is written where the top-level walk can see it.
// B sits inside a tuplet -- a container that owns its own walk and hands out its body's
// DIRECT children, so a mark attached to a note inside it never became a site and the
// statement-level handler that BUILDS a rehearsal mark never ran. B drew NOTHING,
// silently, until 2026-08-30.
//
// ⚠️ THE OTHER HALF OF THIS BOOK'S CLAIM IS NO LONGER WRITABLE, and it is named here
// rather than quietly dropped (RULES §5.4). Until 2026-08-31 the repeat and its two
// endings were written IN THE MUSIC -- `|: d1 | [1. e1@mark("C") ] :| [2. f1@mark("D") ]`
// -- so C and D sat inside INLINE VOLTA containers, the second container family the
// 2026-08-30 fix had to reach. LYS1034 moved repeat structure to the form, so an inline
// ending cannot be written at all and no book can hold a mark inside one. The letters are
// kept (they still exercise the de-dupe below, and they still print) but they now stand in
// ordinary section music: THE CONTAINER HALF OF THE GUARD IS GONE, and the surviving
// container observer is the tuplet.
//
// The `tab melody` line is load-bearing, not decoration: one part scored onto two staves
// is collected once per staff, so the same fix that makes the letters appear prints each
// of them TWICE without the position de-dupe. A one-staff book cannot tell the two apart.
//
// ✔ VERIFIED TO GUARD, not assumed to (session 293), with two poisons that differ:
//   - drop the rehearsal arm from MeasureCollector.CollectArticulations -> B, C and D
//     vanish from this snapshot (A survives, through the old statement path);
//   - drop its `MusicMarkExistsAt` de-dupe -> all four letters double.
// The tracked corpus was BLIND to both: the fix moved 45 of the reader's own books
// (120 restored letters, 0 texts lost) and not one snapshot in the repository.
